1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,555
A soft potato.

2
00:00:01,555 --> 00:00:02,709
A gritty guava.

3
00:00:02,709 --> 00:00:04,117
A strong jute rope.

4
00:00:04,517 --> 00:00:06,305
All three come from plants.

5
00:00:06,305 --> 00:00:10,679
But one feels soft, one feels sandy,
and one is strong enough to tie a

6
00:00:10,679 --> 00:00:11,579
cart.

7
00:00:11,656 --> 00:00:16,172
Diya bit into a guava and asked: what
are these tiny hard grains, and why

8
00:00:16,172 --> 00:00:19,380
is a plant soft in one
place and tough in another?

9
00:00:19,730 --> 00:00:21,364
The answer is tissues.

10
00:00:21,364 --> 00:00:25,402
A tissue is a team of cells
that work together on one job.

11
00:00:25,902 --> 00:00:30,176
By the end, you will know the five
permanent tissues of a plant, and spot

12
00:00:30,176 --> 00:00:31,646
each one by its cells.

13
00:00:32,296 --> 00:00:34,460
First, what does permanent mean here?

14
00:00:34,810 --> 00:00:37,265
Young cells at the tips keep dividing.

15
00:00:37,265 --> 00:00:41,792
When they stop dividing and take up
one job for good, they become permanent

16
00:00:41,792 --> 00:00:42,692
tissue.

17
00:00:42,872 --> 00:00:47,084
A simple tissue is made of one type
of cell: parenchyma, collenchyma and

18
00:00:47,084 --> 00:00:47,984
sclerenchyma.

19
00:00:48,407 --> 00:00:53,224
A complex tissue is made of many types
of cells working as one: xylem and

20
00:00:53,224 --> 00:00:54,124
phloem.

21
00:00:54,551 --> 00:00:58,196
Let's zoom into a young stem
and meet the simple tissues.

22
00:00:58,546 --> 00:01:00,628
Parenchyma is the soft filling.

23
00:01:00,628 --> 00:01:05,011
Its cells have thin walls of cellulose,
often with small spaces between

24
00:01:05,011 --> 00:01:05,911
them.

25
00:01:06,067 --> 00:01:10,221
Parenchyma makes food by photosynthesis,
stores food, and secretes.

26
00:01:10,221 --> 00:01:13,036
The soft inside of a
potato is mostly this.

27
00:01:13,536 --> 00:01:17,327
Collenchyma sits just below
the skin of young dicot stems.

28
00:01:17,327 --> 00:01:21,786
Its walls are thick only at the
corners, with cellulose, hemicellulose

29
00:01:21,786 --> 00:01:22,716
and pectin.

30
00:01:23,216 --> 00:01:27,549
That makes it strong but bendy, to
hold up a young stem, or a leaf stalk,

31
00:01:27,549 --> 00:01:28,754
called the petiole.

32
00:01:29,254 --> 00:01:31,220
Sclerenchyma is the tough one.

33
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:35,863
Its walls are thick and hard with
lignin, and its cells are usually dead.

34
00:01:35,863 --> 00:01:40,601
It comes as long fibres, and short
sclereids, like the grains in a guava.

35
00:01:41,251 --> 00:01:42,921
Now the complex tissues.

36
00:01:42,921 --> 00:01:45,105
Watch them work inside the stem.

37
00:01:45,455 --> 00:01:48,259
Xylem carries water and
minerals up from the roots.

38
00:01:48,259 --> 00:01:52,391
Its tracheids and vessels are dead,
empty tubes with hard, lignified walls.

39
00:01:53,141 --> 00:01:57,370
A vessel is made of many cells
joined end to end, like a pipe.

40
00:01:57,370 --> 00:02:00,100
Vessels are a feature of flowering plants.

41
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:01,608
Gymnosperms lack them.

42
00:02:02,358 --> 00:02:07,189
Xylem also has xylem fibres for strength,
and living xylem parenchyma that

43
00:02:07,189 --> 00:02:08,215
stores food.

44
00:02:08,965 --> 00:02:12,614
Phloem carries food, usually
from the leaves to other parts.

45
00:02:12,614 --> 00:02:14,554
Food flows through sieve tubes.

46
00:02:14,554 --> 00:02:18,166
Their end walls have holes
like a sieve: the sieve plates.

47
00:02:18,916 --> 00:02:21,389
A mature sieve tube has no nucleus.

48
00:02:21,389 --> 00:02:24,169
The companion cell beside it controls it.

49
00:02:24,169 --> 00:02:28,512
Phloem also has phloem parenchyma,
and phloem fibres, like jute.

50
00:02:29,262 --> 00:02:32,537
Now the pair most students
mix up: xylem and phloem.

51
00:02:32,887 --> 00:02:34,694
Both are complex tissues.

52
00:02:34,694 --> 00:02:36,241
Both are long tubes.

53
00:02:36,241 --> 00:02:38,276
Both run from root to leaf.

54
00:02:38,726 --> 00:02:41,363
But xylem carries water,
and its tubes are dead.

55
00:02:41,363 --> 00:02:44,121
Phloem carries food, and
its sieve tubes are alive.

56
00:02:44,771 --> 00:02:45,671
Your turn.

57
00:02:45,573 --> 00:02:47,804
A mature sieve tube has no nucleus.

58
00:02:47,804 --> 00:02:51,261
So who controls it: the
companion cell, or the vessel?

59
00:02:51,261 --> 00:02:52,441
Pause and decide.

60
00:02:55,641 --> 00:02:57,003
The companion cell!

61
00:02:57,003 --> 00:03:01,780
It sits right beside the sieve tube,
and its nucleus runs the sieve tube.

62
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:03,922
A vessel is a dead xylem tube.

63
00:03:04,572 --> 00:03:05,525
Remember this.

64
00:03:05,525 --> 00:03:07,913
One type of cell makes a simple tissue.

65
00:03:07,913 --> 00:03:10,434
Many types of cells make a complex tissue.

66
00:03:11,034 --> 00:03:12,596
Now, a worked example.

67
00:03:12,596 --> 00:03:15,211
Let's match four clues
to the right tissue.

68
00:03:15,561 --> 00:03:19,612
Walls thickened only at the
corners, holding up a young stem.

69
00:03:19,612 --> 00:03:21,039
That is collenchyma.

70
00:03:21,489 --> 00:03:23,522
Hard grains in the pulp of a guava.

71
00:03:23,522 --> 00:03:25,530
That is sclerenchyma: the sclereids.

72
00:03:25,980 --> 00:03:29,046
Thin walls, living cells,
food stored inside.

73
00:03:29,046 --> 00:03:30,402
That is parenchyma.

74
00:03:30,852 --> 00:03:33,404
End walls with sieve
plates, carrying food.

75
00:03:33,404 --> 00:03:34,436
That is phloem.

76
00:03:35,086 --> 00:03:36,548
Now, the exam trap.

77
00:03:36,898 --> 00:03:40,045
Many students think every
plant with xylem has vessels.

78
00:03:40,445 --> 00:03:41,996
But gymnosperms lack vessels.

79
00:03:41,996 --> 00:03:43,795
They move water through tracheids.

80
00:03:43,795 --> 00:03:45,761
Vessels are a feature of angiosperms.

81
00:03:46,411 --> 00:03:47,650
Where is this asked?

82
00:03:47,650 --> 00:03:51,576
As statements: which plants lack
vessels, which cells are dead, and where

83
00:03:51,576 --> 00:03:52,765
sclereids are found.

84
00:03:53,365 --> 00:03:54,462
Exam level now.

85
00:03:54,462 --> 00:03:58,755
Read the four statements, count the
correct ones, and pause to solve.

86
00:04:04,155 --> 00:04:05,466
The answer is B.

87
00:04:05,466 --> 00:04:07,651
Only two statements are correct.

88
00:04:08,151 --> 00:04:09,511
One and two are true.

89
00:04:09,511 --> 00:04:13,344
Sclereids are in guava pulp, and
most monocots lack phloem parenchyma.

90
00:04:13,844 --> 00:04:16,806
Three is false: gymnosperms
lack companion cells.

91
00:04:16,806 --> 00:04:19,915
Four is false: collenchyma
walls are not lignified.

92
00:04:20,515 --> 00:04:22,055
Here is your recap.

93
00:04:22,055 --> 00:04:23,638
Screenshot this card.

94
00:04:26,838 --> 00:04:29,023
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

95
00:04:29,423 --> 00:04:30,323
One.

96
00:04:29,834 --> 00:04:32,775
What makes a tissue simple,
and what makes it complex?

97
00:04:33,675 --> 00:04:34,575
Two.

98
00:04:34,059 --> 00:04:36,258
Where are the walls of
collenchyma thickened?

99
00:04:37,158 --> 00:04:38,058
Three.

100
00:04:37,682 --> 00:04:39,748
Which group of plants lacks vessels?

101
00:04:40,848 --> 00:04:45,215
Next time: why does a leaf feel
waxy, and how does it breathe?

102
00:04:45,215 --> 00:04:47,969
We find out in tissue systems in plants.
